PATCHLOG - iperf3v patch series
===============================

Base:    ESnet iperf, git tag in iperf3v/BASE_TAG (https://github.com/esnet/iperf)
Branch:  fork/<base tag>  (never rewritten; corrections are "fixup!" commits,
         squashed only when the series is ported to the next ESnet tag)
Version: <base tag>-vN, set by AC_INIT (configure.ac and configure)

Each patch is one commit. The prefix of the commit title is its label:

    windows:  Windows/Cygwin port, not proposed upstream as is
    fix:      generic bug fix, must apply alone on the ESnet tag; proposed upstream
    feature:  addition specific to iperf3v
    fork:     identity of the fork (version, docs, tests, CI), never proposed upstream

Upstream status: not proposed / issue #n / PR #n / merged in 3.x (patch dropped
at the next port).

Patches on 3.22.1
-----------------

    windows: dscp: fix Cygwin/Windows header includes
        src/dscp.c - strcasecmp and IP_TOS headers on Cygwin/Win32.
        Upstream: not proposed.

    windows: net: add TransmitFile zero-copy support for Windows/Cygwin
        src/net.c - Nsendfile() through TransmitFile.
        Upstream: not proposed.

    windows: util: report TransmitFile in zerocopy feature string
        src/iperf_util.c - "sendfile / zerocopy" feature line.
        Upstream: not proposed.

    windows: api: add Windows high-resolution timer for rate throttling
        src/iperf_api.c - iperf_check_throttle(): CreateWaitableTimerExW
        HIGH_RESOLUTION + 200us QPC spin, fallback Sleep + QPC spin;
        --debug prints which path is used.
        Upstream: not proposed.

    windows: api: make TOS/DSCP setsockopt non-fatal on Windows
        src/iperf_api.c - IP_TOS / IPV6_TCLASS failures become warnings.
        Upstream: not proposed.

    fix: udp: fix --skip-rx-copy byte accounting
        src/iperf_udp.c - with MSG_TRUNC the received size is the truncated
        size; count blksize instead.
        Upstream: not proposed.

    windows: configure: include <sys/types.h> in TCP_KEEPIDLE check so
    --cntl-ka is enabled on Cygwin
        configure.ac and configure - Cygwin's <netinet/tcp.h> uses u_int16_t
        without including <sys/types.h>, so the check failed and --cntl-ka
        was missing. Edits the generated configure too (no automake on
        Cygwin; AM_MAINTAINER_MODE keeps make from regenerating it).
        Upstream: not proposed.

    fork: identify as iperf3v 3.22.1-v1
        configure.ac, configure, PATCHLOG, README-iperf3v.md, iperf3v/BASE_TAG.

    fork: add Linux netns regression harness
        iperf3v/tests/ - netns/netem/iptables helpers, ci-linux.sh, run_all.sh.

    fix: udp: tolerate out-of-order packets while waiting for the connect
    reply in --bidir mode
        src/iperf_udp.c - iperf_udp_connect(): the out-of-order tolerance of
        -R (PR #1260) also applies to --bidir. netem "delay 30ms 12ms":
        10/20 failed before, 0/20 after (iperf3v/tests/test_bidir_reorder.sh).
        Upstream: not proposed.

    fix: net: return one datagram per read in Nrecv_no_select for UDP
        src/net.c - the read loop filled the whole buffer, so a short UDP
        datagram was concatenated with the following ones (and the read
        blocked until blksize bytes had arrived). Data datagrams fill the
        buffer at once and are unaffected.
        Upstream: not proposed.

    fix: udp: resend the connect message and accept the first reverse
    datagram as an implicit reply
        src/iperf.h, src/iperf_udp.c, src/iperf_udp.h, src/iperf_client_api.c
        - the client resends UDP_CONNECT_MSG until UDP_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SEC
        (30 s). The first resend waits max(1 s, 4 control round trips, measured
        around the parameter exchange), then the wait doubles up to 8 s, so a
        slow reply is not taken for a lost one. In -R/--bidir a data datagram
        counts as the reply. A 4-byte connect message or reply on a stream
        socket is never counted as data, and the server answers a repeated
        connect message on a running receive stream.
        Needs: "fix: net: return one datagram per read" (the server side).
        Against an ESnet server: a lost reply is recovered in -R/--bidir
        (implicit reply); in -u mode the test still fails as before, and a
        resend that reaches an ESnet server which did reply (reply later than
        4 round trips) is read as data and corrupts its loss counters
        (iperf3v/tests/test_slow_reply.sh covers 1.2 s round trips).
        Related upstream: esnet/iperf#1402 (UDP connect retry).
        Upstream: not proposed.

    fix: udp: server answers a repeated connect message on an already
    accepted stream
        src/iperf_udp.c, src/iperf_udp.h, src/iperf_server_api.c - while it
        creates streams, the server also selects on the UDP streams already
        accepted and answers a repeated connect message (lost reply of the
        first stream: --bidir, -P > 1). Needs iperf3v on both ends.
        Needs: the resend fix above.
        Upstream: not proposed.

    fork: check that fix patches apply on the ESnet base tag
        iperf3v/tests/check_fix_patches_apply.sh.

    fork: CI for Linux, Cygwin and network regressions
        .github/workflows/iperf3v-build.yml, iperf3v-regress.yml.

    fork: weekly ESnet watch, issue templates and labels
        .github/workflows/iperf3v-upstream-watch.yml, iperf3v/watch/,
        .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/, iperf3v/setup-labels.sh.

    fork: release packaging for Linux and Windows
        .github/workflows/iperf3v-release.yml, iperf3v/package-linux.sh,
        iperf3v/package-cygwin.sh.

    windows: udp: shared receive socket for UDP servers with several streams
        src/iperf_udp.c, src/iperf_udp.h, src/iperf.h, src/iperf_api.c,
        src/iperf_server_api.c - Windows hands every UDP datagram to the
        first socket bound to the port and never to a later one, connected or
        not (a connected first socket drops other peers' datagrams). So a
        Windows server failed with more than one stream socket (-P > 1,
        --bidir) since the first ventana build. On Windows/Cygwin only
        (IPERF_UDP_SHARED_RCV), the listener stays unconnected as the shared
        receive socket; each stream gets its own socket bound to the same port
        and connected to the client, used to send; the first receiving
        stream's thread reads the shared socket and dispatches each datagram
        by sender address (each stream's statistics keep a single writer); a
        connect message repeated by an accepted client is answered on its
        stream's socket. Linux and other systems keep the ESnet code.
        Test: iperf3v/tests/windows_loopback.sh (CI, Cygwin job).
        Upstream: not proposed (ESnet does not support Windows).

    fork: version 3.22.1-v2

Known issues
------------

    After a lost reply, the server's statistics start up to one resend
    interval before the client sends: receiver duration is longer and the
    bitrate lower by that much.

Build checks (every port)
-------------------------

    configure must print:
        checking whether compiling and linking against OpenSSL works... yes
        checking TCP_KEEPIDLE socket option... yes
    and "iperf3 --help" must list --cntl-ka.

    Cygwin (C:\cygwin64, packages gcc-core make libssl-devel zlib-devel):
        ./configure --with-openssl=/usr && make -j8
        Runtime DLLs shipped next to iperf3v.exe: cygwin1.dll, cygcrypto-3.dll,
        cygz.dll (check with: ldd iperf3v.exe | grep -vi windows)
